{"retractions" [[17592186362393 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?"] [17592186362393 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186362395 17592186362396 17592186362397 17592186362398 17592186362399] "immutable_fields" [[17592186362394 :answer-field/id] [17592186362394 :answer-field/key] [17592186362394 :answer-field/type] [17592186362394 :answer-field/correct] [17592186362394 :answer-field/choices]]}
